# PATCH: 006-attest-requests  (v2 — nonce + body binding)
#
# PURPOSE: Attach a per-request ECIES attestation token to every outbound
#          HTTP(S) request so that the DenBrowser proxy (Pingora-based; see
#          proxy/) can verify that traffic originated from a genuine DenBrowser
#          browser build, *and* that the captured headers cannot be replayed
#          to issue a different request from curl.
#
# KEY MODEL (private key lives on the proxy, never in the browser):
#
#   Proxy holds:    ECDSA P-256 private key  (long-lived, rotate on breach)
#   Browser embeds: matching SPKI public key (new one per browser build)
#
#   For each new DenBrowser release:
#     1. Run  scripts/gen-attest-key.sh  to generate a fresh keypair.
#     2. Add the new public key bytes to kProxyPublicKeyDer[] and rebuild.
#     3. Deploy the new private key to the proxy (proxy/build/proxy-private.pem).
#     4. Remove the old public key from the proxy's allowlist — that build's
#        traffic is now rejected even if the binary is still in circulation.
#
#   The browser binary contains ONLY the public key, which is not secret.
#   Extracting it from the binary gives an attacker nothing useful because
#   you cannot forge an encrypted token without the proxy's private key.
#
# WHY THIS DOES NOT AFFECT PKI / OAUTH:
#   These headers are an independent application-layer channel added on top
#   of TLS.  TLS handshakes, certificate verification, and all OAuth flows
#   are completely untouched.  The proxy strips X-DenBrowser-* after verification;
#   downstream servers never see them.
#
# CRYPTOGRAPHIC PROTOCOL (ECIES — Elliptic-Curve Integrated Encryption):
#
#   Per-request browser side:
#     1. Generate a fresh ephemeral EC P-256 keypair (ephem_priv, ephem_pub).
#     2. ECDH(ephem_priv, proxy_pub) → 32-byte raw shared secret Z.
#     3. ANSI X9.63 KDF: SHA-256(Z || 0x00000001) → take first 16 bytes
#        as an AES-128 key  K_enc.
#     4. Generate random 12-byte IV.
#     5. Generate random 16-byte nonce  N.
#     6. Compute  body_hash = sha256(request_body)  in lowercase hex.
#        EXCEPTION — large uploads: if the upload body exceeds kMaxBoundBodyBytes
#        (or the stream cannot be cloned to hash it), the body field is set to
#        the literal "unbound" instead of a hash.  The proxy then streams the
#        body straight through without buffering it.  Origin, replay, timestamp,
#        and method/host/path binding still apply; only per-body integrity is
#        dropped for these large uploads.  Because "unbound" lives inside the
#        GCM-authenticated plaintext, a client cannot forge it or downgrade a
#        normal (bound) request without the proxy's private key.
#     7. Build the canonical plaintext:
#          "denbrowser-attest:v2\n<base64(N)>\n<ts>\n<host>\n<method>\n<path>\n<body_hash>"
#          (the final field is the literal "unbound" for large uploads)
#     8. AES-128-GCM-Encrypt(K_enc, IV, plaintext) → ciphertext || 16-byte tag.
#     9. token = base64( ephem_pub(65) || IV(12) || ciphertext_and_tag )
#
#   Headers added:
#     X-DenBrowser-Ts:    Unix timestamp, whole seconds.
#     X-DenBrowser-Nonce: base64 of the 16-byte nonce.
#     X-DenBrowser-Token: base64 of the encrypted blob (above).
#
#   Proxy verification (Rust, see proxy/src/attest.rs):
#     1. Read X-DenBrowser-Ts; reject (403) if |now – ts| > 30 s.
#     2. Decode X-DenBrowser-Nonce; reject if length ≠ 16 bytes.
#     3. Peek the per-nonce replay cache; reject (403) if already present.
#     4. Decode token, ECDH + KDF, AES-128-GCM decrypt.
#     5. Compare every plaintext field (prefix, nonce, ts, host, method, path)
#        against the request the proxy actually received.  Reject on mismatch.
#     6. If the body_hash field is a hash: buffer the request body, hash it,
#        compare to the body_hash field of the plaintext, reject on mismatch.
#        If it is the "unbound" sentinel: commit the nonce now and stream the
#        body straight through without buffering (large uploads).
#     7. Atomically insert the nonce into the replay cache and forward.
#
# WHAT THIS PROTECTS AGAINST  (vs. the v1 design):
#
#   v1 plaintext bound only  (ts, host).  An attacker with local capture
#   (e.g. WireGuard MITM with an injected trust-store CA) could lift the
#   Ts/Token pair off a captured request and replay it against any path
#   on the same host for the next ~30 s — including different methods,
#   different bodies, and different endpoints.
#
#   v2 binds  (nonce, ts, host, method, path, body_hash)  inside the
#   GCM-authenticated plaintext, *and* the proxy maintains a per-nonce LRU
#   keyed by the 16 random bytes that survives for slightly longer than the
#   drift window.  As a result:
#
#     - Captured headers cannot be used for a different request — every
#       request semantic is in the authenticated plaintext.
#     - Captured headers cannot be re-used even for the *same* request —
#       the proxy refuses any nonce it has already accepted.
#     - A TOCTOU race between two parallel replays of the same captured
#       token loses in phase-2 commit (atomic check-and-insert).
#
#   The only attack the protocol still allows is replaying the exact same
#   request to the exact same path with the exact same body before the
#   legitimate request reaches the proxy — a race the attacker cannot win
#   from a separate process under normal conditions, and which the LRU
#   eliminates entirely once either request lands.
#
# FILES ADDED:
#   netwerk/base/DenBrowserAttest.h
#   netwerk/base/DenBrowserAttest.cpp
#
# FILES MODIFIED:
#   netwerk/protocol/http/nsHttpChannel.cpp
#       — include DenBrowserAttest.h, call denbrowser::AddAttestHeaders() at
#         end of SetupChannelForTransaction() before return NS_OK,
#         passing mUploadStream so the body can be hashed.
#   netwerk/base/moz.build
#       — add DenBrowserAttest.cpp to UNIFIED_SOURCES.

